-- title:
--   Hecke matrix entries as divisor correspondence coefficients, ℓ ≠ p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $K$ a field of characteristic $p$ with decidable equality, and $M, s$ nonzero natural numbers. Let $X$ be a supersingular two-level datum `SSLevelDatum p K M s`, which packages the inclusions of $j(q^M)$ and $j(q^s)$ into the level-$Ms$ function field, integrality witnesses `fstIntegral`, `sndIntegral` for the two degeneracy legs and, for every level $N$ and every nonzero $\ell$, integrality witnesses `legsIntegral` for both maps `heckeAlphaC K N ℓ` (the inclusion of the level-$N$ function field into the roof `charLDegeneracyRoof K N ℓ`, the subfield of Laurent series generated by $j(q)$, $j(q^N)$, $j(q^\ell)$, $j(q^{N\ell})$) and `heckeBetaC K N ℓ` (the substitution $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$), together with preservation of supersingular places by the two legs, an Atkin–Lehner involution preserving supersingular places, and Frobenius data satisfying the Kronecker congruence. Let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \neq p$. Then two statements hold. First, assuming the roof `charLDegeneracyRoof K (M * s) ℓ` has principal divisors, for all supersingular places $y, x$ of level $Ms$ (elements of `ssPlaces p (M * s) K`) the $(y,x)$ entry of the matrix `X.edgeHecke ℓ` equals the coefficient at $y$ of the divisor obtained from the prime divisor $[x]$ by pull-back along `heckeBetaC` followed by push-forward along `heckeAlphaC`, with the datum's integrality witnesses. Second, the same identity holds at level $M$ with `X.vertexHecke ℓ` and the roof `charLDegeneracyRoof K M ℓ`.
--
--   This identifies the $\ell$-th Hecke matrix on supersingular points, at both the edge level $Ms$ and the vertex level $M$, with the matrix of the classical correspondence $\alpha_*\beta^*$ on divisors of the degeneracy roof, for $\ell$ away from the characteristic. It is the bridge between the combinatorially defined Hecke family on supersingular places and divisor-theoretic computations, and is used in the analysis of Hecke row sums, adjointness relations, and the character lattice of the component group.
-- source:
